window.docContents[176].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Green-Kernels-and-Poisson-Representation--The-Ball-Kernel-and-Dirichlet-Problem":{"contents":"Kelvin inversion places an image pole outside the ball, so subtracting the\nimage fundamental solution produces a kernel that is harmonic away from the\nphysical pole and vanishes on the sphere. The negative outward normal\nderivative  is the Poisson kernel, and the\nassociated Poisson extension is regular, harmonic, recovers its boundary\nvalues, and is the unique such solution.\n\nTo make the construction concrete, consider the three-dimensional unit ball.\nThe fundamental solution is . For an interior pole\n with , the Kelvin image is , which lies\noutside the ball since . The Green's function is then\n\n\n\nOn the boundary sphere , the identity  makes\nthe two terms cancel, confirming . The outward normal derivative\nof  is negative; its negative gives the Poisson kernel\n\n\n\nThis explicit formula shows that the Poisson kernel is positive, singular as\n, and has mass one over the sphere. For the ball of\nradius , scaling replaces  by  and introduces the\nappropriate dimensional normalization (Evans, 2010, pp. 40–41;\n, Theorem 2.6).\n\nIn positive dimension, write  when .\nThe unit-ball Green kernel is the total functionwith the continuous  branch written explicitly in Lean. The first\nvariable is the physical pole and the second is the field point; in the\none-dimensional branch,  and  denote their sole coordinates. For\n the kernel is the Kelvin construction of Evans §2.2.4; for  it\nis the Dirichlet kernel of  on .\n\nThe defining property of a Green kernel is that it vanishes when the field point\nreaches the boundary. For the Kelvin construction this is the inversion identity\n on the unit sphere, which\ngives the two fundamental-solution terms arguments of equal norm.\n\nFor , an interior pole and a boundary field point satisfy\n\nWrite  for the Kelvin image of a nonzero pole.\nFor , the inversion identity givesThus the physical and image arguments of the fundamental solution have the\nsame norm, so the two terms in the Green kernel cancel. The proof treats\ndimension two and dimensions at least three separately because the\nfundamental solution is logarithmic in the former and a negative power in the\nlatter. If , both formulas reduce directly using\n.\n\nBoundary vanishing concerns the sphere; harmonicity concerns the interior. For a\nnonzero interior pole the image  lies outside the closed ball, so both\nfundamental-solution terms are harmonic in  throughout\n. Harmonicity off the pole together with vanishing on\nthe sphere makes  a Green kernel.\n\nFor , , and , the field-variable\nLaplacian vanishes:\n\nExpand the Kelvin-inversion formula.  Each fundamental-solution term is\nharmonic away from its pole, and the geometric hypotheses keep both relevant\npoles outside the point where the field-variable Laplacian is evaluated.\n\nThe Poisson kernel is the negative outward normal derivative\n on the sphere, in which the two\nfundamental-solution terms combine into a single closed expression. We take\nthat closed form, scaled to a ball of radius , as the definition of the\nkernel, following Evans's computation of ; the theorems\nabout the Poisson extension work from the formula directly.\n\nFor a positive radius , the ball Poisson kernel isIts boundary-value interpretation uses  and\n; the Lean definition is total.\n\nIntegrating the kernel against boundary data over the sphere gives the Poisson\nextension. The definition pulls the -independent prefactor\n outside the integral, leaving\n as the integrand; this is the form in which the\nextension is differentiated.\n\nThe Poisson extension integrates the prescribed boundary datum over the\nradius- sphere with the normalization used by the ball Poisson kernel.\nAnalytic theorems about this total function supply positivity of ,\ncontinuity of the data, and the appropriate interior or boundary hypotheses.\n\nThe prefactor form and the kernel form of the extension are equal as total\nfunctions, with no hypotheses on , , or , so later theorems may pass\nbetween them freely.\n\nThe prefactor form of the ball extension is algebraically equal to the sphere\nintegral of .\n\nInsert the explicit formula for  into the sphere integral. The\nfactor  is independent of  and\ncan be pulled outside, leaving exactly the prefactor definition of the\nPoisson extension.\n\nThe Poisson extension is meaningful only inside the ball. The Dirichlet\ncandidate glues it to the datum on the sphere and outside, and attaching the\ndata definitionally rather than through a limit is what lets boundary equality\nbe exact and uniqueness be stated as an equation on the closed ball.\n\nThe boundary-attached Dirichlet candidate is the total functionThus it is the Poisson extension in the open ball and agrees definitionally\nwith the prescribed ambient datum outside it.\n\nClassical harmonicity requires two derivatives, but the same kernel gives every\nderivative order in the interior. Near a fixed interior point, a smooth cutoff\nremoves the inverse-distance singularity without changing the sphere integral.\nMathlib's convolution regularity theorem then gives smoothness.\n\nIn positive dimension, for a positive radius and a continuous ambient\nrepresentative of the boundary datum, differentiation under the sphere\nintegral gives a  extension on the open ball. The linked theorems\nallow every regularity order , both at individual\ninterior points and throughout the ball. The choice  supplies the\nregularity needed by the classical Laplacian statement.\n\nFix an interior point and choose a smaller neighborhood whose distance from\nthe boundary sphere is positive. The differences between points in this\nneighborhood and points on the sphere lie in a compact annulus away from zero.\nReplace the inverse-distance kernel by a smooth compactly supported kernel\nthat agrees on this annulus. Its convolution with the boundary datum is\nsmooth and agrees locally with the original potential. Multiplying by the\nsmooth Poisson prefactor preserves every derivative order.\n\nGiven the two derivatives, the Laplacian of the extension is the sphere integral\nof , and for  the kernel is harmonic in\n. The theorem carries this pointwise cancellation through the integral.\n\nIn positive dimension, if ,  is\ncontinuous, and , then the coordinate Laplacian of the Poisson\nextension vanishes:The proof differentiates the kernel twice, uses the common norm-square\nidentities, and performs the resulting sphere-integral cancellation.\n\nUse the two derivative formulas justified by interior regularity and sum their\ndiagonal terms. After writing all denominators over\n, the norm-square identities for boundary points\n make the numerator cancel. Integrating the zero kernel\nLaplacian yields .\n\nInterior regularity and the vanishing Laplacian are exactly the two clauses of\nthe harmonicity predicate. Combining them for the boundary-attached candidate,\nwhich agrees with  on the open ball, gives the existence half of the\nDirichlet theorem at the  level.\n\nIn positive dimension, for  and continuous ambient data , the\nboundary-attached solution satisfiesInterior  regularity holds as well, by the\nsmoothness theorem.\n\nInside the ball, the boundary-attached solution is definitionally the Poisson\nextension. Apply its interior  theorem and its pointwise Laplacian-zero\ntheorem; together these are exactly the classical harmonicity predicate on\n.\n\nHarmonicity inside the ball settles nothing about the boundary condition.\nBoundary recovery rests on the kernel being positive with mass one and\nconcentrating at  as , so that the Poisson\nintegral of continuous data converges to .\n\nIn positive dimension, if  and  is continuous, thenThe proof first shows\n as  through  for every\n; the attached total solution then gives exact\nboundary equality.\n\nSubtract  inside the Poisson integral and use that the kernel has\nmass one. Continuity makes this difference uniformly small near ,\nwhile positivity and concentration of the kernel control the complementary\npart. This proves the interior limit; the piecewise definition supplies exact\nboundary values and glues the two continuous pieces on the closed ball.\n\nUniqueness comes from the maximum principle. The difference of two solutions\nis harmonic in the ball, continuous on its closure, and zero on the sphere, and\ntwo applications of the weak maximum principle force it to vanish. The\nmean-value property and the derivative estimates use this theorem to identify\na harmonic function with its Poisson extension.\n\nIn positive dimension, let , let  be continuous, and let\n. Ifthen\n\nSubtract the two harmonic functions.  Their difference is continuous up to\nthe boundary and vanishes there, so the weak maximum principle applied to the\ndifference and its negative forces it to vanish on the ball.\n\nEvans states these properties of  together as Poisson's formula for the\nball.\n\nIn positive dimension, for  and continuous , the Poisson formula\ngives a field  such thatfor every .\n\nThe three clauses are the interior Laplacian identity, interior \nregularity, and pointwise boundary convergence of the Poisson integral.\n\nWith uniqueness, the Poisson formula gives the only solution of the ball\nDirichlet problem with continuous data.\nFor model domains with explicit geometry, the classical and\npotential-theoretic routes therefore give the same answer.\nFor general domains, uniqueness instead follows from the maximum principle\nalone, without an explicit representation\n(, Theorem 2.5).\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity\u0009Green Kernels and Poisson Representation","header":"5.3.1.1. The Ball Kernel and Dirichlet Problem","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Green-Kernels-and-Poisson-Representation--The-Ball-Kernel-and-Dirichlet-Problem"},"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations","header":"3.3. Coordinates and Boundary Calculus","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Computation-with-a-mathematical-conclusion":{"contents":"Verified computer algebra has its own history in Lean. Lewis and Wu's\nLean–Mathematica interface exchanges expressions in both directions and\nreconstructs Lean proofs for selected computations returned by Mathematica\n. The external algebra system acts as an\nuntrusted source of candidates. This distinction also matters for an\nantiderivative: finding a formula and proving that its derivative equals the\nintegrand, on the required domain, are separate steps.\n\nThe Lean community asked for computer algebra inside proofs well before PDEAtlas. The\n2020 CAS-tactics wishlist\nasked for expression manipulation that sits naturally inside a Lean proof. In a\n2023 Lean Zulip discussion,\nKaiyu Yang proposed a transparent PDE solver, and the discussion asked how to\nturn that idea into a concrete plan. Our answer is a set of symbolic procedures\nwhose results come with proofs over the analytic objects a PDE argument already\nuses.\n\nHex provides a more recent comparison inside Lean itself. Its released libraries\ncover verified computational algebra, including polynomial algorithms, lattice\nbasis reduction, and algebraic numbers, with separate Mathlib correspondence\nlayers where available . This exact algebra supports\ncomputations with finite mathematical objects. PDEAtlas connects symbolic\ndifferentiation, supported antiderivatives, and certified enclosures to analytic\nfunctions and PDE residuals. Its procedures return theorems over the analytic\ndefinitions used by the surrounding proofs, with domain and regularity\nconditions retained in the statements. A residual calculation can then become\na proved identity inside an analytic argument.\n\nValidated numerics contributes a complementary guarantee. Boldo, Clément,\nFilliâtre, Mayero, Melquiond, and Weis verified a C implementation of a\none-dimensional acoustic wave scheme, accounting for both discretization and\nfloating-point roundoff errors\n. Their analysis connects the program,\nthe discrete scheme, and the continuous equation. The distinction explains why\ncertifying arithmetic alone cannot establish the accuracy of a PDE solver:\nstability and approximation estimates must connect those levels.\n\nThe Rocq Interval library automates bounds on real expressions and integrals\n. In Lean, LeanCert turns interval,\nroot, and integral certificates into mathematical conclusions\n. Its interval and quadrature tools overlap\nclosely with CEGA: a finite calculation produces a bound that an analytic proof\ncan use. In PDEAtlas, CEGA keeps the function and its domain attached to that\nbound, so an estimate for a residual on one box can feed into a larger PDE\nargument. LeanCert offers kernel reduction and a default native evaluation\nroute; the latter additionally relies on Lean's compiler and runtime.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.4. Computation with a mathematical conclusion","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Computation-with-a-mathematical-conclusion"}});